<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Somali Youth League</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1943">1943</a>–<a href="/years/1969">1969</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as SYL</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> Youth League was the leading nationalist party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>. Founded in Mogadishu in 1943 as the Somali Youth Club and renamed in 1947, it campaigned for the unity and independence of the Somali territories. It won the elections of 1956 and 1959 in the Italian-administered trust territory, led Somalia to independence in 1960 and dominated its governments until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siad-barre">Siad Barre</a>'s military coup of 1969 banned all parties.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/somali-youth-league.jpg" alt="Somali Youth League" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thommy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Somali_Youth_League.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The league grew up while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Italian Somaliland</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and administered it militarily from 1941 to 1950. It began with thirteen founding members on 15 May 1943. Its founding principle was that members should not mention or disclose their clan, in an effort to promote national unity: the thirteen founders, although they came from four of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>'s five major clans, refused to say which. Better educated police officers and civil servants were allowed to join, and its members drew inspiration from the religious rebellions of figures such as Uways al-Barawi, Sheikh Hassan Barsane and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammed-abdullah-hassan">Mohammed Abdullah Hassan</a>.</p><p>Under its secretary general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdullahi-issa">Abdullahi Issa</a> it opened offices in Italian and British Somaliland, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogaden">Ogaden</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-frontier-district">Northern Frontier District</a>. Its stated aims were to unite all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> territories, to open modern education to all, to give the Somali language a standard national orthography, to safeguard Somali interests and to oppose the restoration of Italian rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The league campaigned against any Italian return under the slogan &quot;No matter what the color, a wolf is always a wolf&quot; and asked instead for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> trusteeship over all lands mainly inhabited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somalis</a>. When the United Nations gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> the trusteeship of the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-somaliland">Italian Somaliland</a> in November 1949, the league first considered armed resistance but chose a more temperate course, demanding that Arabic rather than Italian be the official language and that no officials of the fascist era return. In the first years of the Italian administration many league officials were demoted, removed or imprisoned, and demonstrations were strongly repressed.</p><p>On 1 July 1960 the former Italian territory united with British Somaliland to form the Somali Republic, with Aden Abdullah Osman Daar as its first president and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdirashid-ali-shermarke">Abdirashid Ali Shermarke</a> as prime minister. In the elections of 30 March 1964 the league won 69 of the 123 seats in parliament, and in March 1969 it returned to power under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-haji-ibrahim-egal">Muhammad Haji Ibrahim Egal</a>. President Shermarke was assassinated the same year, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siad-barre">Siad Barre</a>'s Supreme Revolutionary Council then dissolved parliament and suspended the constitution.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The league's founding on 15 May 1943 is commemorated each year in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a> as Somali Youth Day, with official celebrations held across the country in honour of its members and their part in the road to independence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Somali Youth League” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Somali Youth League</b>, the leading nationalist party, won the elections of 1956 and 1959, and Somalis took over ministries under Prime Minister Abdullahi Issa.</blockquote><a href="/state/trust-territory-of-somaliland">Trust Territory of Somaliland</a></li></ul></section>
